[PERSISTENT EXPERIENCE GUIDANCE]
## Consolidated Experience from Similar Tasks
Pickup attempts can fail when preconditions are not met: the target object may be missing, out of reach, or the robot may not be correctly positioned. Always verify that the object exists, is reachable, and that all preconditions are satisfied before attempting pickup, and confirm the grasp afterward instead of assuming success. Move attempts can fail when obstacles block the intended path. Before moving, scan the immediate direction and overall route, and if blocked, clear the obstruction or choose an alternative route rather than issuing a forward move. These lessons apply directly to putting away multiple objects: each object must be located, reached, picked up, carried, and placed with ongoing verification and obstacle-aware navigation.

## Preliminary Thinking
1. Locate the Cup, Bowl, Pot, and Mug, and identify their designated storage locations. 2. Survey the environment for obstacles and plan collision-free paths between the objects and storage areas. 3. For each object, position the robot so the object is detected and reachable, then attempt pickup and verify success before proceeding. If pickup fails, check for missing detection, out-of-reach placement, or robot misalignment. 4. Carry the object toward storage while checking the path ahead; if an obstacle blocks movement, reroute or clear it rather than continuing. 5. Place the object in its storage location and confirm the placement. 6. Repeat the locate-verify-pickup-carry-place-confirm cycle for all four items, handling any failures by rechecking preconditions rather than blindly retrying.
[/PERSISTENT EXPERIENCE GUIDANCE]